Early life[edit]Cole was raised in 
Rockport, Massachusetts. Her mother, Stephanie Cole, a mixed media artist, was an elementary school art teacher; her father, Jim Cole, was a professor of biology and ecology at 
Salem State College and played bass in the 
polka band "Johnny Prytko and the Connecticut Hi-Tones".
[11][12] Her older sister Irene played piano.
[11] She has stated she has Irish, Italian and Polish ancestry.
[13]She attended 
Rockport High School, where she was president of her senior class and performed in school theatrical productions such as 
South Pacific.
[14] Cole then attended 
Berklee College of Music in 
Boston, where she studied jazz singing and improvisation with 
Bob Stoloff.
[11][15] She sang jazz standards at lounges and nightclubs.
[16] One of the school projects was with Vox One, a chorus group at Berklee that later turned to pro as well.
[17] She was offered a record deal by the jazz label 
GRP Records, but decided to turn it down.
[11][15]After graduating Berklee, she moved to 
San Francisco and began working on song ideas.
[18] She lived with three roommates and ate meagerly, building up her home studio and writing down song ideas including one that later became "
Where Have All the Cowboys Gone?"
[19] Impressing label president 
Terry Ellis with her demo performances, she signed with his 
Imago Records in 1992, and was coached by veteran artist's manager John Carter on the album project that would become 
Harbinger.
